WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.527
If you have studied real estate anywhere else, you have met the
phrase dual agent.

2
00:00:05.727 --> 00:00:07.039
Put it down.

3
00:00:07.239 --> 00:00:13.822
Search Chapter 1101 of the Occupations Code and the words dual agent
and dual agency are not in it.

4
00:00:13.822 --> 00:00:18.780
Texas built a different role, with a different name
and different rules.

5
00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:21.538
Here is the problem it solves.

6
00:00:21.738 --> 00:00:26.594
One brokerage lists a house and also represents the
buyer who wants it.

7
00:00:26.794 --> 00:00:29.598
The seller’s floor is protected.

8
00:00:29.998 --> 00:00:32.885
The buyer’s ceiling is protected.

9
00:00:33.085 --> 00:00:37.282
Full agency to both is not a hard case; it is an impossible one.

10
00:00:37.282 --> 00:00:41.644
Section 1101.561(b) settles it: a broker must agree to act as an

11
00:00:41.644 --> 00:00:50.533
intermediary under this subchapter if the broker agrees to represent
in a transaction a buyer or tenant and a seller or landlord.

12
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:51.645
Must.

13
00:00:51.845 --> 00:00:54.416
The consumer notice states the mechanics.

14
00:00:54.416 --> 00:01:02.271
To act as an intermediary between the parties, the broker must first
obtain the written agreement of each party to the transaction.

15
00:01:02.271 --> 00:01:06.056
The written agreement must state who will pay the broker and, in

16
00:01:06.056 --> 00:01:11.769
conspicuous bold or underlined print, set forth the broker’s
obligations as an intermediary.

17
00:01:11.769 --> 00:01:15.768
Written, from each party, stating who pays, with the prohibitions

18
00:01:15.768 --> 00:01:20.409
emphasized—conspicuous, and no point size, because
the statute names none.

19
00:01:20.609 --> 00:01:22.820
Then what the broker does.

20
00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:27.373
Must treat all parties to the transaction impartially and fairly.

21
00:01:27.373 --> 00:01:31.571
And may, with the parties’ written consent, appoint a different

22
00:01:31.571 --> 00:01:35.925
license holder associated with the broker to each party, owner and

23
00:01:35.925 --> 00:01:44.088
buyer, to communicate with, provide opinions and advice to, and carry
out the instructions of each party to the transaction.

24
00:01:44.088 --> 00:01:46.653
Two consents doing two different jobs.

25
00:01:46.653 --> 00:01:50.466
So a Texas brokerage on both sides runs in one of two configurations.

26
00:01:50.466 --> 00:01:58.226
Without appointments: the broker is intermediary, impartial, carrying
information—and nobody in the brokerage advises either party.

27
00:01:58.226 --> 00:02:02.039
With appointments: the broker stays impartial, and each party has a

28
00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:06.722
licensee who may give opinions and advice during
negotiations, to that party only.

29
00:02:06.922 --> 00:02:08.894
The difference is advice.

30
00:02:09.294 --> 00:02:11.332
It is not confidentiality.

31
00:02:11.532 --> 00:02:14.317
Because confidentiality is identical in both.

32
00:02:14.317 --> 00:02:18.495
The intermediary must not, unless specifically authorized in writing

33
00:02:18.495 --> 00:02:22.254
to do so by the party, disclose: that the owner will accept a price

34
00:02:22.254 --> 00:02:26.223
less than the written asking price; that the buyer or tenant will pay

35
00:02:26.223 --> 00:02:30.122
a price greater than the price submitted in a written offer; and any

36
00:02:30.122 --> 00:02:33.882
confidential information or any other information that a party

37
00:02:33.882 --> 00:02:39.382
specifically instructs the broker in writing not to disclose, unless
required to do so by law.

38
00:02:39.382 --> 00:02:43.908
The seller’s floor and the buyer’s ceiling stay on
their own sides of the wall.

39
00:02:44.108 --> 00:02:46.333
Two things to carry out of here.

40
00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:49.025
An appointment is not a release.

41
00:02:49.225 --> 00:02:52.941
Section 1101.651(d) binds the broker and every license holder the

42
00:02:52.941 --> 00:02:57.387
broker appoints, so an appointed agent advises inside a
confidentiality cage.

43
00:02:57.387 --> 00:03:01.170
And the notice is a summary: the statute carries qualifiers the form

44
00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:08.403
does not print, including that confidentiality never covers
information materially relating to the condition of the property.

45
00:03:08.603 --> 00:03:11.094
It protects negotiating position.

46
00:03:11.494 --> 00:03:13.785
It has never protected a defect.
